Mesoscale tensile blind predictions — AM-Bench 2022 (@mat-am-tensile: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_72TG9WD15YP5DCTVCCX26W7FND
Orion L. Kafka, Jake Benzing, Newell Moser, Li-Anne Liew, Jordan Weaver and Nikolas Hrabe. *Additive Manufacturing Benchmark 2022 Subcontinuum Mesoscale Tensile Challenge (CHAL-AMB2022-04-MeTT) and Summary of Predictions* (2023), DOI 10.1007/s40192-023-00307-5. [Official publication](https://www.nist.gov/publications/additive-manufacturing-benchmark-2022-subcontinuum-mesoscale-tensile-challenge-chal), [complete manuscript](https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=956226), [referenced dataset](https://data.nist.gov/od/id/mds2-2587).

Read the entire paper and available methods/data. Explain what the investigators supplied before predictions, what remained hidden, and what each measured quantity actually represents. Compare a model's success across several observables instead of ranking it by one convenient response.

The 23-page manuscript's introduction, tensile-method and uncertainty passages, conclusions and availability statements were inspected on 30 October 2026. Its small IN625 specimens contain few grains; that response is not automatically a bulk design property. Published stress and strain conversions have stated assumptions. The paper explicitly says some DIC processing code and raw data were withheld for legal reasons. Do not describe the entire experiment as independently reproducible from public inputs.

NIST-authored government contributions have their stated US copyright treatment; preserve other component credits. Dataset metadata and complete raw files still need inspection for any chosen reproduction. Nothing is bundled.

